---

<DocsLayout
    title="Architecture"
    toc={toc}
>
    <h1>How the engine works<span class="title-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h1>
    <p class="lead">
        ChunkHound turns your entire codebase, git history, and the web into
        one cited answer — indexed on your machine, researched by affordable
        models — because the engine thinks locally before a model writes a word.
    </p>

    <EngineStages />

    <h2 id="context">Where the engine sits</h2>
    <p>
        ChunkHound is a local process that owns a local index. Agents reach it
        through MCP; shells reach it through the CLI. It reads from your
        repository, git, and the web, and it calls model providers for
        embeddings, reranking, and writing. The index stays put; only request
        content travels.
    </p>
    <SystemContextDiagram />

    <h2 id="pipeline">One question, one cited answer</h2>
    <p>
        Sources reach the index by different routes: code, diffs, and pages are
        not the same shape, so each has its own parsers that chunk it
        accordingly. Once each is chunked and embedded, though, the research
        that follows is the same for all of them. The engine runs two phases.
        <strong>Exploration</strong> searches by meaning and by pattern, then
        interleaves reranking with a utility model&rsquo;s follow-ups, imports, and
        gaps until the results converge. <strong>Synthesis</strong> turns that
        converged set into one cited answer through map-reduce, constant
        extraction, and a fact ledger. Because the phases meet at one stable boundary — a converged
        result set — the engine stays adaptable: the same exploration and
        synthesis serve a code repository, a git history, or the open web.
    </p>
    <SourcesFlowDiagram />

    <h2 id="cutoff">The adaptive cutoff</h2>
    <p>
        Most systems keep a fixed top-k, or scale the budget to repository size.
        ChunkHound reads the score distribution of the query itself and cuts at
        its natural elbow — the point that falls farthest from the line between
        the best and worst scores. When the curve has no clear elbow the result
        set is kept whole rather than truncated to a guess, while the follow-up
        thresholds that guide exploration fall back to the median score. A broad
        question keeps more results; a narrow one keeps less, with no
        per-repository tuning.
    </p>
    <ElbowChart />
    <p class="result">No fixed top-k. The score curve sets the cutoff.</p>

    <h2 id="tokens">Cheap tokens, expensive tokens</h2>
    <p>
        Deep research makes a lot of small model calls — expanding the query,
        writing follow-up questions, classifying results. ChunkHound routes that
        volume to a cheap utility model and reserves the synthesis model for a
        single pass over results that have already been narrowed, clustered, and
        reduced.
    </p>
    <TokenRoutingDiagram />

    <h2 id="index">From files to a current index</h2>
    <p>
        Before a question can be answered, files have to become a current index —
        and at repository scale that is a throughput problem. The engine is
        written in Rust: it scans in parallel, diffs against the stored index,
        and overlaps parsing, embedding, and storage so all three run at once.
        Database writes, compaction, crash recovery, and the embedding HTTP calls
        are native too. Python still supplies tree-sitter parsing and the
        embedding fallback for providers without a native client, called from the
        engine — a holdover from when the whole system was Python. The project is
        gradually leaving it behind, aiming to retire Python entirely.
        Speed is only half the point. In a codebase built entirely by AI agents,
        Rust's static types are the other half — the compiler rejects whole
        classes of mistake before a single test runs.
    </p>
    <IndexFlowDiagram />

    <h2 id="why">Why it is shaped this way</h2>
    <p>
        Every choice above follows from one constraint: answer questions larger
        than the context window, on hardware you own. A local index makes that
        possible. One retrieval path makes every source comparable. The adaptive
        cutoff spends context only where the evidence is. The two-model split
        keeps each answer cheap. And a Rust engine keeps the index current at
        repository scale.
    </p>
</DocsLayout>
